{{also|chen|Chen|chēn|chén|chěn|chèn}}
==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l/de|-ken|lang=de}} {{i|colloquial in northern Germany and Westphalia}}

===Etymology===
Native to the Central German dialects, ''-chen'' has widely replaced the southern {{term|-lein|lang=de}} in standard usage. Cognate with Low German {{term|-ken|lang=de}} and Dutch {{term|-tje|lang=nl}} and {{term|-ke|lang=nl}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/çən/|[çən]|[çn̩]|lang=de}}

===Suffix===
{{head|de|suffix|cat2=noun-forming suffixes|g=n|plural|-chen}}

# {{non-gloss definition|the most common German suffix to create a [[diminutive]] form; e.g., ''[[Hund]]'' &rarr; ''Hünd'''chen''''' ("little dog")}}

====Usage notes====
* Words that have the suffix ''-chen'' are always neuter, whatever gender the basic word.
* Words ending in ''-ch'' (and some others) use the double suffix {{term|-elchen|lang=de}}, e.g. {{term|Strichelchen|lang=de}} from {{term|Strich|lang=de}}. However, the suffix ''-lein'' is quite common for these words in writing ({{term|Strichlein|lang=de}}).

